{
	title: 'Interrupt active rounds on ordinary submission',
	spec: 'Ordinary submitted messages immediately interrupt an active model round on the host, identically for terminal, peer and remote/web clients. Receipt is durably appended to the visible inbox before aborting, with command IDs retained for resend deduplication. No tool call still pending at receipt may subsequently dispatch: even a completed provider response waiting for dispatch must observe cancellation. Preserve partial assistant output, usage and completed tool results. Abort the provider and running foreground tool using the round signal; wait for their settlement before replacing that signal and making the next provider request. Suppressed tool calls have valid error results, and provider replay pairs every recorded call before delivering the new prompt. Interrupted rounds remain inside the same logical turn, not a user pause; history alone suffices for takeover recovery. Messages received while cancellation settles coalesce in receipt order into one prompt without loss or duplication. Explicit Escape, closing a tab and deliberate final-process quit suppress the automatic restart; pending messages remain visible and durable until continued or another ordinary submission. Alt-Enter/queue never interrupts and runs after turn completion, or remains visibly waiting while paused. Advisory send never interrupts; send steer:true shares ordinary submission interruption semantics. Advisory/queued messages from other sessions never undo an explicit pause. Login/retry waits are cancelled by ordinary submission and re-evaluated by the new request, rather than leaving the text stranded until the old wait ends. Slash commands continue their own existing immediate command behavior; submitting text is not delayed to a provider round boundary. Architecture state rules and the older inbox task describe these same semantics. Deterministic tests exercise provider abortion, preservation of partial output, late tool-call suppression, foreground tool settlement, multiple messages, explicit pause/close priority, queue/advisory behavior, retry waits and durable deduplication/recovery.',
	status: 'done',
